WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.758
Fifty million payments are spread over 40 partitions, each on its own machine.

2
00:00:07.758 --> 00:00:11.908
To place a payment, the store hashes its id to pick a partition.

3
00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:14.975
Now ask for merchant M-0042's payments today.

4
00:00:14.975 --> 00:00:22.213
They're scattered over nearly every partition, so the store asks all 40, and each one reads everything it holds.

5
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.244
A store like this finds data only by its key.

6
00:00:25.244 --> 00:00:29.731
So before you choose keys, write down the questions your code will ask.

7
00:00:29.933 --> 00:00:33.531
One: a merchant's payments for one day, newest first.

8
00:00:33.531 --> 00:00:35.771
Two: a single payment, by its id.

9
00:00:35.967 --> 00:00:40.475
For question one, make the partition key the merchant and the day.

10
00:00:40.475 --> 00:00:44.641
All of M-0042's payments for today now land in one partition.

11
00:00:44.833 --> 00:00:48.399
Inside it, a sort key, the time, keeps them in order.

12
00:00:48.399 --> 00:00:52.569
Newest first is one slice: start at the end, and read back 20.

13
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.193
So each payment has a two-part address.

14
00:00:55.193 --> 00:00:59.917
The partition key picks the partition, and the sort key says where inside it.

15
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:04.649
Question two gives only a payment id, which isn't in that address.

16
00:01:04.649 --> 00:01:09.543
So the store keeps a second copy, keyed by id: one partition, one item.

17
00:01:09.543 --> 00:01:12.024
It costs one more write per payment.

18
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:18.703
Then a question nobody planned for arrives: failed payments over 500 euros, at any merchant.

19
00:01:18.703 --> 00:01:25.419
No key matches, so the store reads all 40 partitions, 50 million payments, to return a handful.

20
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:31.298
Write the questions down first, and give each one a key that makes it one read in one partition.

21
00:01:31.298 --> 00:01:34.147
A question you didn't plan for reads everything.
